  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...The latest surface analysis shows a stalled \nsurface front south of the Crystal Coast this evening. \nMeanwhile, a secondary front (possibly an elevated 850mb front),\nis progressing SW across ENC. Boundary remains to our south \nfor much of Sunday, but temperatures have trended slightly lower\ntomorrow with likely lingering convective debris and \npotentially earlier start of convection. No Heat Advisories \nissued for tomorrow, although spots from Lenoir County southward\nmay get close to 105 for an hour or two. Higher confidence in \ndangerous heat remains on Monday as the front lifts north and \nre- entrenches ENC in a very hot airmass with 1000-850 mb \nthicknesses once again climbing above 1430m and widespread Tds \nin the mid to upper 70s. This could nudge max heat index values \nabove 110 in many areas. We continue to encourage anyone \nplanning on spending time outdoors to stay hydrated and to limit\nexposure to the heat, if able.\n\nAnother frontal boundary quickly pushes across NC on Monday \nevening before stalling along the coast. Like this weekend, SW \ncounties will likely remain hot and humid while Albemarle and \nvicinity see some modest relief. A more widespread cooldown, \nhowever, is on the horizon late next week as a stronger cold \nfront dives out of Canada and across the eastern CONUS.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The latest surface analysis shows a stalled \nsurface front south of the Crystal Coast this evening. \nMeanwhile, a secondary front (possibly an elevated 850mb front),\nis progressing SW across ENC. It's this boundary where some \nlingering convection is ongoing, mainly from Carteret County \noffshore. Until the boundary clears the area, there will \ncontinue to be a risk of showers and a few thunderstorms, mainly\nalong, and just inland of, the Crystal Coast. However, the risk\nof strong thunderstorms is expected to remain low the remainder\nof the night.\n\nFrontal boundary begins to lift northward on Sunday, shifting \nmore robust convection threat closer to VA but still leaving a \nfavorable environment for sea breeze activity, as well as some \nupstream threats once again making their way towards the area in\nthe evening. Evolution will be heavily driven by mesoscale \nfactors owing to continued higher than average forecast \nuncertainty owing to large spread in CAMs.\n\nAnother frontal boundary looks to approach on Monday. There \nwill still be plenty of details to hammer out but both AI and \ntrusted deterministic NWP guidance point to at least another \nmarginal risk of strong to severe storms associated with this \nboundary. It will stall along our coast next week, keeping our \narea in an unsettled pattern through most of the work week.",
